      "wordCount": 780,
      "passage": "[A] It took at least 3,000 years for humans to learn how to domesticate the wild tomato and cultivate it for food. Now two separate teams in Brazil and China have done it all over again in less than three years. And they have done it better in some ways, as the re-domesticated tomatoes are more nutritious than the ones we eat at present.\n\nThis approach relies on the revolutionary CRISPR genome editing technique, in which changes are deliberately made to the DNA of a living cell, allowing genetic material to be added, removed or altered. The technique could not only improve existing crops, but could also be used to turn thousands of wild plants into useful and appealing foods. In fact, a third team in the US has already begun to do this with a relative of the tomato called the groundcherry.\n\nThis fast-track domestication could help make the world's food supply healthier and far more resistant to diseases, such as the rust fungus devastating wheat crops.\n\n'This could transform what we eat,' says Jorg Kudla at the University of Munster in Germany, a member of the Brazilian team. 'There are 50,000 edible plants in the world, but 90 percent of our energy comes from just 15 crops.'\n\n'We can now start the process again, and create different kinds of food,' says Kudla.\n\n[B] The wild tomato, which is native to the Andean region of South America, produces tiny pea-sized berries with a flavor that is pleasant but unremarkable. Humans discovered that if they repeatedly cultivated those plants that had random genetic mutations that made them sweeter, larger, and easier to harvest, they could transform the wild plant into a crop.\n\nOver time, this selective breeding changed the wild tomato into the familiar fruit we know today. However, this process had downsides. By selecting only for traits that made the plants higher-yielding and easier to harvest, ancient breeders inadvertently bred out other useful qualities, such as resistance to disease and tolerance to drought or salty soils.\n\nWhat's more, intensive selective breeding significantly narrowed the genetic diversity of modern tomatoes. As a result, today's commercial varieties are vulnerable to devastating pests and diseases, requiring copious amounts of pesticides to thrive.\n\n[C] Kudla's team used CRISPR to edit six genes in a wild tomato called Solanum pimpinellifolium, which is known for its tolerance to bacterial spot disease and high salt levels. The researchers targeted genes that control fruit size, shape, number, and nutrient content.\n\nThe results were astonishing. The modified plants produced ten times more tomatoes than wild plants, and the fruits were three times larger. Crucially, the edited tomatoes retained the wild ancestor's natural resistance to bacterial disease and salt tolerance.\n\nEven better, the team managed to boost the level of lycopene, an antioxidant that gives tomatoes their red color and has been linked to lower risks of heart disease and certain cancers. The re-domesticated tomatoes contained five times more lycopene than standard tomatoes.\n\n'They taste quite good,' says Kudla. 'A bit stronger, slightly aromatic, with a very nice flavour.'\n\n[D] Meanwhile, Caixia Gao and her colleagues at the Chinese Academy of Sciences in Beijing took a similar approach with another wild tomato variety. They modified four genes related to fruit size and quality.\n\nThe team succeeded in engineering tomatoes that were not only resistant to salt stress but also had a markedly higher vitamin C content than modern commercial tomatoes.\n\n'This study provides a practical blueprint for the de novo domestication of wild plants into staple crops,' Gao noted in their published findings.\n\n[E] Other scientists are enthusiastic about the broader implications. Joyce Van Eck at the Boyce Thompson Institute in New York believes genome editing can unlock the culinary potential of neglected wild relatives. Her lab has already used CRISPR to modify the groundcherry (Physalis pruinosa), making the plant more compact and preventing its fruits from falling off before they ripen.\n\n'We can engineer entirely new crops from scratch,' says Van Eck. 'Instead of spending centuries selecting traits, we can achieve identical or superior results in just a few years.'\n\nChannapatna Prakash, a crop geneticist at Tuskegee University, agrees: 'Fast-track domestication allows us to rescue genetic diversity, fortify crops against climate shocks, and produce far tastier and more resilient food for a growing global population.'",
