      "level": "Band 7.0 - 8.5",
      "duration": "20 phút",
      "wordCount": 750,
      "passage": "[A] Scientific discovery is popularly believed to result from the sheer genius of such intellectual stars as naturalist Charles Darwin and theoretical physicist Albert Einstein. Our view of such unique contributions to science often disregards the person's prior experience and the efforts of their lesser-known predecessors. Conventional wisdom also places great weight on insight in promoting breakthrough scientific achievements, as if ideas spontaneously pop into the heads of geniuses at arbitrary moments.\n\n[B] To examine this assumption, we must carefully consider what is meant by 'insight'. In everyday language, the term typically refers to a sudden flash of comprehension, an epiphany where the solution to a vexing problem appears fully formed. In psychological science, however, the concept is far more nuanced. Many cognitive scientists argue that what feels like sudden insight is actually the culmination of a protracted, iterative process of problem-solving. Ideas do not emerge from an intellectual vacuum; rather, they are assembled through trial, error, incremental adjustments, and accumulated knowledge.\n\n[C] Take the case of Charles Darwin. His theory of evolution by natural selection is frequently hailed as an unparalleled stroke of biological genius. Yet Darwin himself acknowledged that the core idea did not strike him out of the blue. Instead, it was shaped over many years of meticulous observation aboard HMS Beagle, extensive correspondence with pigeon breeders, and his reading of Thomas Malthus's essay on population growth. Similarly, Albert Einstein's formulation of special relativity was deeply indebted to the experimental work of Albert Michelson and Edward Morley, as well as the mathematical formulations of Hendrik Lorentz and Henri Poincaré.\n\n[D] The history of science is replete with instances where groundbreaking theories were built on foundational ideas proposed by predecessors who made partial discoveries or even outright errors. In the early 20th century, the British astronomer John Nicholson hypothesized the existence of several proto-elements in celestial bodies, including 'coronium' and 'nebulium'. While Nicholson's chemical ideas were ultimately proven incorrect, his mathematical calculations regarding the quantum structure of atoms provided the critical stepping stone for Niels Bohr to develop his landmark model of the hydrogen atom in 1913.\n\n[E] Furthermore, accidental discoveries and fortunate coincidences frequently play a pivotal role. The invention of the iconic Post-It note occurred when Spencer Silver, a chemist at 3M, developed a low-tack, reusable adhesive that seemed commercially useless until his colleague Art Fry realized it could be used to anchor bookmarks in his church hymnal without damaging the pages. In other domains, simple necessity provokes novel solutions. Jockey Tod Sloan revolutionized thoroughbred horse racing when he adopted the 'crouch' riding style—shortening his stirrups and perching high over the horse's neck to minimize wind resistance—a radical departure from the upright posture traditionally mandated by equestrian authorities.\n\n[F] When we examine innovation across nature, we find striking parallels. Charles Darwin's Law of Natural Selection posits that biological adaptation proceeds through blind variation and selective retention: random mutations arise without prior knowledge of their utility, and the environment filters out disadvantageous variations. In 1898, the American psychologist Edward Thorndike proposed the Law of Effect, which describes how animal learning follows a remarkably similar principle. In his puzzle box experiments, cats learned to escape not by deliberate reasoning or foresight, but through erratic exploration. Behaviors that produced successful outcomes were reinforced, whereas ineffective actions were discarded.\n\n[G] Both natural selection and the Law of Effect reveal that remarkable adaptations and novel behaviors can emerge through iterative variation and selection without any conscious design or forethought. Acknowledging the evolutionary character of discovery does not diminish the prestige of great innovators. Rather, it demystifies human creativity, showing that breakthrough discoveries are the hard-won fruit of continuous exploration, iterative refinement, and the courage to learn from trial and error.",
