        {
          "id": "cam17_t2_p2",
          "title": "Passage 2: A second attempt at domesticating the tomato",
          "wordCount": 780,
          "content": "[A] It took at least 3,000 years for humans to learn how to domesticate the wild tomato and cultivate it for food. Now two separate teams in Brazil and China have done it all over again in less than three years. And they have done it better in some ways, as the re-domesticated tomatoes are more nutritious than the ones we eat at present.\n\nThis approach relies on the revolutionary CRISPR genome editing technique, in which changes are deliberately made to the DNA of a living cell, allowing genetic material to be added, removed or altered. The technique could not only improve existing crops, but could also be used to turn thousands of wild plants into useful and appealing foods. In fact, a third team in the US has already begun to do this with a relative of the tomato called the groundcherry.\n\nThis fast-track domestication could help make the world's food supply healthier and far more resistant to diseases, such as the rust fungus devastating wheat crops.\n\n'This could transform what we eat,' says Jorg Kudla at the University of Munster in Germany, a member of the Brazilian team. 'There are 50,000 edible plants in the world, but 90 percent of our energy comes from just 15 crops.'\n\n'We can now start the process again, and create different kinds of food,' says Kudla.\n\n[B] The wild tomato, which is native to the Andean region of South America, produces tiny pea-sized berries with a flavor that is pleasant but unremarkable. Humans discovered that if they repeatedly cultivated those plants that had random genetic mutations that made them sweeter, larger, and easier to harvest, they could transform the wild plant into a crop.\n\nOver time, this selective breeding changed the wild tomato into the familiar fruit we know today. However, this process had downsides. By selecting only for traits that made the plants higher-yielding and easier to harvest, ancient breeders inadvertently bred out other useful qualities, such as resistance to disease and tolerance to drought or salty soils.\n\nWhat's more, intensive selective breeding significantly narrowed the genetic diversity of modern tomatoes. As a result, today's commercial varieties are vulnerable to devastating pests and diseases, requiring copious amounts of pesticides to thrive.\n\n[C] Kudla's team used CRISPR to edit six genes in a wild tomato called Solanum pimpinellifolium, which is known for its tolerance to bacterial spot disease and high salt levels. The researchers targeted genes that control fruit size, shape, number, and nutrient content.\n\nThe results were astonishing. The modified plants produced ten times more tomatoes than wild plants, and the fruits were three times larger. Crucially, the edited tomatoes retained the wild ancestor's natural resistance to bacterial disease and salt tolerance.\n\nEven better, the team managed to boost the level of lycopene, an antioxidant that gives tomatoes their red color and has been linked to lower risks of heart disease and certain cancers. The re-domesticated tomatoes contained five times more lycopene than standard tomatoes.\n\n'They taste quite good,' says Kudla. 'A bit stronger, slightly aromatic, with a very nice flavour.'\n\n[D] Meanwhile, Caixia Gao and her colleagues at the Chinese Academy of Sciences in Beijing took a similar approach with another wild tomato variety. They modified four genes related to fruit size and quality.\n\nThe team succeeded in engineering tomatoes that were not only resistant to salt stress but also had a markedly higher vitamin C content than modern commercial tomatoes.\n\n'This study provides a practical blueprint for the de novo domestication of wild plants into staple crops,' Gao noted in their published findings.\n\n[E] Other scientists are enthusiastic about the broader implications. Joyce Van Eck at the Boyce Thompson Institute in New York believes genome editing can unlock the culinary potential of neglected wild relatives. Her lab has already used CRISPR to modify the groundcherry (Physalis pruinosa), making the plant more compact and preventing its fruits from falling off before they ripen.\n\n'We can engineer entirely new crops from scratch,' says Van Eck. 'Instead of spending centuries selecting traits, we can achieve identical or superior results in just a few years.'\n\nChannapatna Prakash, a crop geneticist at Tuskegee University, agrees: 'Fast-track domestication allows us to rescue genetic diversity, fortify crops against climate shocks, and produce far tastier and more resilient food for a growing global population.'"
        },
        {
          "id": "cam17_t2_p3",
          "title": "Passage 3: Insight or evolution?",
          "wordCount": 750,
          "content": "[A] Scientific discovery is popularly believed to result from the sheer genius of such intellectual stars as naturalist Charles Darwin and theoretical physicist Albert Einstein. Our view of such unique contributions to science often disregards the person's prior experience and the efforts of their lesser-known predecessors. Conventional wisdom also places great weight on insight in promoting breakthrough scientific achievements, as if ideas spontaneously pop into the heads of geniuses at arbitrary moments.\n\n[B] To examine this assumption, we must carefully consider what is meant by 'insight'. In everyday language, the term typically refers to a sudden flash of comprehension, an epiphany where the solution to a vexing problem appears fully formed. In psychological science, however, the concept is far more nuanced. Many cognitive scientists argue that what feels like sudden insight is actually the culmination of a protracted, iterative process of problem-solving. Ideas do not emerge from an intellectual vacuum; rather, they are assembled through trial, error, incremental adjustments, and accumulated knowledge.\n\n[C] Take the case of Charles Darwin. His theory of evolution by natural selection is frequently hailed as an unparalleled stroke of biological genius. Yet Darwin himself acknowledged that the core idea did not strike him out of the blue. Instead, it was shaped over many years of meticulous observation aboard HMS Beagle, extensive correspondence with pigeon breeders, and his reading of Thomas Malthus's essay on population growth. Similarly, Albert Einstein's formulation of special relativity was deeply indebted to the experimental work of Albert Michelson and Edward Morley, as well as the mathematical formulations of Hendrik Lorentz and Henri Poincaré.\n\n[D] The history of science is replete with instances where groundbreaking theories were built on foundational ideas proposed by predecessors who made partial discoveries or even outright errors. In the early 20th century, the British astronomer John Nicholson hypothesized the existence of several proto-elements in celestial bodies, including 'coronium' and 'nebulium'. While Nicholson's chemical ideas were ultimately proven incorrect, his mathematical calculations regarding the quantum structure of atoms provided the critical stepping stone for Niels Bohr to develop his landmark model of the hydrogen atom in 1913.\n\n[E] Furthermore, accidental discoveries and fortunate coincidences frequently play a pivotal role. The invention of the iconic Post-It note occurred when Spencer Silver, a chemist at 3M, developed a low-tack, reusable adhesive that seemed commercially useless until his colleague Art Fry realized it could be used to anchor bookmarks in his church hymnal without damaging the pages. In other domains, simple necessity provokes novel solutions. Jockey Tod Sloan revolutionized thoroughbred horse racing when he adopted the 'crouch' riding style—shortening his stirrups and perching high over the horse's neck to minimize wind resistance—a radical departure from the upright posture traditionally mandated by equestrian authorities.\n\n[F] When we examine innovation across nature, we find striking parallels. Charles Darwin's Law of Natural Selection posits that biological adaptation proceeds through blind variation and selective retention: random mutations arise without prior knowledge of their utility, and the environment filters out disadvantageous variations. In 1898, the American psychologist Edward Thorndike proposed the Law of Effect, which describes how animal learning follows a remarkably similar principle. In his puzzle box experiments, cats learned to escape not by deliberate reasoning or foresight, but through erratic exploration. Behaviors that produced successful outcomes were reinforced, whereas ineffective actions were discarded.\n\n[G] Both natural selection and the Law of Effect reveal that remarkable adaptations and novel behaviors can emerge through iterative variation and selection without any conscious design or forethought. Acknowledging the evolutionary character of discovery does not diminish the prestige of great innovators. Rather, it demystifies human creativity, showing that breakthrough discoveries are the hard-won fruit of continuous exploration, iterative refinement, and the courage to learn from trial and error."
        }
