      "passage": "[A] The extinct thylacine, also known as the Tasmanian tiger, was a marsupial that bore a superficial resemblance to a dog. Its most distinguishing feature was the 13–19 dark brown stripes across its back, beginning at the rear of the body and extending onto the tail. The thylacine's average nose-to-tail length for adult males was 162.6 cm, compared to 153.7 cm for females.\n\nThe thylacine appeared to occupy most types of terrain except dense rainforest, with open eucalyptus forest thought to be its prime habitat. In terms of feeding, it was exclusively carnivorous, preying on kangaroos, wallabies, small mammals, and birds. Unlike dogs, which rely primarily on speed when chasing prey, the thylacine probably depended mainly on scent when hunting, relentlessly pursuing its quarry over long distances until the animal became exhausted.\n\nLike other marsupials, female thylacines possessed a backward-opening pouch where the young spent their first few months of life suckling milk until they were developed enough to survive outside.\n\n[B] The thylacine was once widespread across mainland Australia and New Guinea, as evidenced by fossil remains and Aboriginal rock art. However, it disappeared from the mainland roughly 2,000 to 3,000 years ago, almost certainly due to competition from dingoes, which were introduced by Asian seafarers. Because dingoes were never introduced to the southern island of Tasmania, a viable thylacine population survived there into modern historical times.\n\n[C] When European pastoralists arrived in Tasmania in the early 19th century, they established extensive sheep farms. From the 1830s onwards, pastoral companies and the colonial government blamed thylacines for killing livestock and introduced bounties, paying hunters cash rewards for every dead carcass. Over the next century, thousands of thylacines were systematically slaughtered.\n\nBy the 1920s, the thylacine had become exceptionally rare in the wild. Habitat fragmentation, diseases such as canine distemper introduced by domestic dogs, and hunting collectively pushed the species to the brink of extinction.\n\n[D] The last known wild thylacine was captured in 1933 and lived at the Hobart Zoo in Tasmania, where it was filmed pacing in its enclosure. Tragically, on the night of 7 September 1936, the animal died of exposure after being locked out of its sheltered sleeping quarters during severe weather.\n\nJust 59 days prior to its death, the Tasmanian government had finally granted the species official protective status—far too late to save it. In 1986, after half a century without verified sightings, the thylacine was officially declared extinct by international scientific authorities.",
