      "wordCount": 780,
      "passage": "[A] Over the past half century, economists, development bankers, and policymakers have treated universal education as the holy grail of national economic development. The prevailing dogma holds that building schools, training teachers, and extending formal schooling generates human capital, which in turn inevitably accelerates labor productivity, technological innovation, and gross domestic product (GDP).\n\nHowever, historical and statistical evidence tells a far more complicated story. In many developing nations, massive state investments in secondary education have expanded diplomas exponentially without generating proportional economic prosperity, resulting instead in widespread graduate underemployment.\n\n[B] To scrutinize the relationship between literacy, schooling, and economic prosperity, economic historian Sheilagh Ogilvie analyzed an extraordinarily rich historical database from southwestern Germany spanning three centuries, from 1600 to 1900. During this period, the German region of Württemberg established one of the earliest universal public schooling systems in Europe, compelling every village boy and girl to attend school and master basic reading.\n\nSurprisingly, Ogilvie found that early high literacy rates in German agricultural villages did not automatically trigger an industrial revolution or increase average earnings. In traditional agrarian communities, reading religious catechisms or singing church hymns conferred minimal practical economic advantage when the surrounding economy was constrained by rigid guild monopolies and feudal regulations.\n\n[C] In fact, when archival court records and village council proceedings are examined, we discover that educational mandates often served social and religious discipline rather than economic innovation. In 1601, church elders in the village of Ebhausen hauled a local weaver before the magistrates because he refused to attend church to hear the annual sermon condemning usury and laziness, receiving a severe financial fine. Compulsory schooling in early modern Europe was largely instituted by ecclesiastical and state authorities to inculcate moral obedience, respect for hierarchy, and religious orthodoxy.\n\n[D] Educational investments only ignited self-sustaining economic growth when they interacted with open economic institutions: secure property rights, competitive markets, free labor mobility, and the freedom to commercialize inventions. In regions where feudal guilds retained the legal authority to suppress technological innovation, restrict trade, and prohibit women from entering skilled crafts, higher literacy produced stagnant wages.\n\n[E] Furthermore, when researchers study individuals with stable career trajectories, the economic returns of education vary dramatically depending on institutional context. In dynamic, meritocratic economies, education equips workers with cognitive adaptability and specialized skills. In corrupt or rent-seeking economies, diplomas merely serve as credentials for securing unproductive civil service sinecures.\n\n[F] The historical lesson is unmistakable: schooling is a necessary foundation, but it is never sufficient on its own. Unless educational expansion is accompanied by institutional reforms that foster competitive enterprise, legal protections, and genuine opportunities for social mobility, classrooms alone cannot engineer an economic miracle.",
