      "wordCount": 720,
      "passage": "[A] On a December weekend in Las Vegas, grandmaster Timur Gareyev achieved an astonishing cognitive feat that set a new Guinness World Record. Blindfolded and seated on an exercise bike, Gareyev played 48 simultaneous games of chess against formidable opponents across an unbroken 19-hour marathon. He won 35 games, drew 7, and lost only 6, navigating an astronomical combinatorial maze entirely inside his mind.\n\n[B] Blindfold chess has a storied heritage spanning over a millennium. Medieval Islamic masters routinely demonstrated blindfold prowess, and in the 18th century, French master François-André Philidor astonished European nobility by playing three opponents simultaneously without looking at the boards. In 1947, the flamboyant Argentine grandmaster Miguel Najdorf established a record of 45 simultaneous blindfold games in São Paulo. Yet Gareyev's 48-board exhibition elevated mental endurance and spatial visualization to an unprecedented summit.\n\n[C] What internal mechanisms enable a human brain to hold 48 evolving, 64-square battlegrounds in working memory without confabulating pieces or confusing positions? Cognitive psychologists and neuroscientists have long debated whether chess savants possess superhuman working memory capacity or whether their ability stems from domain-specific perceptual chunking.\n\n[D] To probe this mystery, a neuroscience research team from the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) subjected Gareyev to comprehensive neurocognitive batteries and functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) scans. First, researchers administered standardized psychological assessments testing his general working memory. Gareyev was asked to memorize and recall long strings of random numbers in forward and reverse order. Surprisingly, his performance on standard digit-span tasks was completely average, demonstrating that his remarkable memory is not a generic, omnipotent mental faculty.\n\n[E] Rather, when Gareyev plays chess, brain imaging revealed extraordinary functional connectivity within his visual cortex and parieto-occipital networks. Instead of memorizing isolated pieces, Gareyev encodes chess positions as holistic, meaningful configurations—a phenomenon known as 'chunking'. He translates the abstract algebraic coordinates communicated verbally by arbiters into vivid dynamic mental landscapes.\n\n[F] Furthermore, fMRI scans indicated exceptional communication between regions governing visual imagery and episodic memory. Gareyev described assigning distinct sensory narratives, emotional tones, and metaphorical colors to individual games. As he pedaled steadily on his stationary bicycle to sustain cerebral blood flow, his brain seamlessly switched between 48 distinct cognitive maps without interference.\n\n[G] Gareyev's achievements illuminate the profound plasticity of the human brain. Rather than reflecting an innate genetic anomaly, his mastery represents the culmination of relentless mnemonic practice, sophisticated cognitive chunking strategies, and physical conditioning designed to optimize mental stamina.",
