      "wordCount": 2240,
      "passage": "[A] In Madagascar, subsistence farming communities face persistent food insecurity due to insect pests that ravage rice harvests. Rice is the cornerstone of Malagasy agriculture, supplying over half of all caloric intake across the island. Yet rice crops are perennially targeted by swarms of destructive insects, particularly the black paddy beetle and the grass webworm.\n\nTraditionally, farmers attempted to protect their crops by spraying synthetic chemical pesticides. However, pesticides are prohibitively expensive for rural families, and their indiscriminate application poses grave health hazards to human communities, poisons aquatic wildlife in irrigation waterways, and inevitably leads to pest resistance.\n\n[B] Fortunately, Malagasy farmers may have an unexpected, natural ally in the night skies: bats. In a landmark field study led by Dr. Ricardo Rocha from the University of Cambridge and the University of Lisbon, an international research team investigated whether insectivorous bats could provide a safe, effective, and completely free biological pest control service in Madagascar's paddy fields.\n\nThe research was conducted on the fringes of Ranomafana National Park in eastern Madagascar. To understand bat feeding habits, Rocha's team deployed specialized ultrasonic audio detectors across rice paddies and surrounding forest canopies. The sensors recorded the distinctive ultrasonic echolocation calls emitted by hunting bats, allowing the researchers to quantify bat activity across different agricultural landscapes.\n\n[C] Crucially, the researchers collected bat droppings beneath common roosting sites in village houses and extracted fecal DNA. Using advanced molecular barcoding, the team sequenced the DNA fragments of insects consumed by six distinct species of bats.\n\nThe genetic analysis revealed that bats consumed vast quantities of devastating agricultural pests. Fecal samples contained DNA from the black paddy beetle, the grass webworm, the sugarcane cicada, and the citrus looper. Even more remarkably, the bats feasted heavily on mosquitoes known to transmit human malaria and lymphatic filariasis, providing an invaluable public health dividend.\n\n[D] Bats are voracious nocturnal predators. Because of their high metabolic rates, bats consume up to their entire body weight in insects every single night. Dr. Rocha calculated that a single colony of insectivorous bats can devour hundreds of millions of insect pests annually, significantly reducing caterpillar and beetle damage to rice paddies without costing farmers a single penny.\n\n[E] However, bats face widespread cultural prejudice in Madagascar. Many rural communities associate bats with dark omens or witchcraft, considering them 'unclean' animals. Bats frequently roost in the roofs of domestic houses and school buildings, generating noise and unpleasant odors that prompt residents to burn bat roosts or exterminate colonies.\n\n[F] To counteract these misconceptions, conservation biologists are working directly with local communities to construct artificial bat houses—wooden roosting boxes mounted on tall poles in the middle of rice fields. These bat houses encourage colonies to relocate away from village dwellings while positioning the animals right where they are needed most: directly above the crop fields where nocturnal pest insects emerge.",
