      "passage": "[A] For centuries, human beings assumed that dreaming was a uniquely human gift—a nocturnal manifestation of our advanced consciousness, symbolic imagination, and linguistic capability. However, modern neurobiological research reveals that many non-human animals experience sleep states remarkably similar to our own, complete with brainwave patterns indicating rich mental experiences during slumber.\n\n[B] The primary neurological signature of dreaming in humans is Rapid Eye Movement (REM) sleep. During REM sleep, the brain is intensely active: electrical oscillations recorded via electroencephalography (EEG) closely resemble the chaotic, desynchronized patterns of wakefulness. Meanwhile, the body enters temporary muscle paralysis, preventing sleepers from physically acting out their dreams.\n\nBiologists have discovered REM sleep across nearly all mammalian species and birds. Marine mammals like dolphins and seals display unihemispheric sleep, shutting down only one half of their brain at a time while keeping the other half awake to surface for air and monitor predators. In terrestrial mammals, however, both hemispheres plunge into deep REM sleep simultaneously.\n\n[C] In pioneering research at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), neuroscientist Matthew Wilson recorded the electrical firing of individual hippocampal neurons in laboratory rats as they navigated complex circular mazes to find food rewards. Wilson discovered that distinct clusters of neurons fired in precise sequences corresponding to specific locations within the maze.\n\nAstonishingly, when the rats subsequently entered slow-wave and REM sleep, the exact same hippocampal place cells fired in the identical sequential order—at virtually the same speed—as when the animals had been actively running the maze hours earlier. The rats were mentally replaying their spatial journeys step-by-step.\n\n[D] Subsequent experiments expanded to other species. When sleeping songbirds undergo REM sleep, neurobiologists recorded neural bursts in the brain's song control nucleus that mirrored the precise motor notes of their territorial songs. The birds were actively practicing their vocal repertoires in their sleep.\n\nSimilarly, behavioral observations of sleeping cuttlefish and octopuses show sudden, rhythmic body twitching and rapid shifts in skin pigmentation patterns and textures while dormant—visual displays that these cephalopods normally deploy only when hunting prey or signaling distress to rivals during waking life.\n\n[E] Why do animals dream? Evolutionary biologists suggest that dreaming sleep is critical for memory consolidation, neural plasticity, and behavioral simulation. By replaying waking experiences in a safe, offline environment, animal brains organize spatial maps, integrate new problem-solving tactics, and reinforce survival skills without expending physical energy or facing real predators.",
