      "wordCount": 790,
      "passage": "[A] In recent years, rapid advancements in artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning, and generative language models have triggered both intoxicating utopian forecasts and apocalyptic warnings about the future of human labor. Optimists proclaim that AI will liberate humanity from monotonous drudgery, catapulting productivity to dizzying heights. Skeptics, meanwhile, caution that intelligent algorithms will precipitate unprecedented mass unemployment, hollow out white-collar professions, and exacerbate social inequality.\n\n[B] To understand the realistic trajectory of AI in the workplace, labor economists urge a careful distinction between tasks and jobs. A job comprises a bundle of diverse tasks. While narrow AI excels at automating routine, well-structured, rule-based computational tasks—such as parsing spreadsheets, reviewing boilerplate legal contracts, or scanning radiologic X-rays—it remains notoriously deficient in tasks demanding deep contextual reasoning, emotional empathy, physical dexterity in unpredictable environments, and moral judgment.\n\n[C] In their influential study on algorithmic substitution, economists Daron Acemoglu and Pascual Restrepo emphasize that technological automation produces two opposing forces: the displacement effect and the reinstatement effect. The displacement effect occurs when machines directly substitute for human labor in existing tasks, suppressing wages and reducing headcounts. Conversely, the reinstatement effect arises when technological breakthroughs generate entirely new, complex tasks and industries that necessitate human expertise.\n\n[D] Historically, the reinstatement effect has consistently outweighed displacement over the long term. The mechanization of 19th-century agriculture and the automation of 20th-century automotive assembly lines initially destroyed millions of manual livelihoods. Yet over subsequent decades, new industries—software development, aviation, healthcare, tourism, and telecommunications—emerged, creating tens of millions of high-paying jobs that our ancestors could not have conceived.\n\n[E] However, transition periods are notoriously agonizing. The current wave of AI differs fundamentally from historical automation in its speed, global scope, and penetration into cognitive domains. In previous technological revolutions, displaced farmhands and textile weavers could transition into factory assembly or service jobs that required minimal intellectual retraining. In contrast, automating cognitive tasks leaves displaced administrative clerks and junior analysts facing an imposing skills chasm that cannot be bridged overnight.\n\n[F] Furthermore, the economic benefits of AI risk becoming intensely monopolized by a handful of mega-corporations that possess the proprietary data, computing clusters, and algorithmic talent needed to build frontier models. Without proactive antitrust regulation and robust public investment in education, AI could amplify the concentration of corporate wealth while eroding worker bargaining power.\n\n[G] Ultimately, the narrative of inevitable technological unemployment is a fatalistic fallacy. Technology does not determine destiny; policy, governance, and institutional design do. If societies invest boldly in lifelong re-skilling, strengthen labor protections, and guide AI deployment toward augmenting rather than replacing human ingenuity, AI can serve as a powerful engine for broad-based human flourishing.",
