      "wordCount": 730,
      "passage": "[A] Across the vast, windswept steppes of Central Asia roams one of Earth's most peculiar and ancient creatures: the saiga antelope (Saiga tatarica). Instantly recognizable by its bulbous, downward-pointing inflatable nose—an extraordinary anatomical adaptation that filters out blowing dust during arid summer migrations and warms frigid sub-zero air before it reaches the lungs in brutal winters—the saiga has survived since the Ice Age, when it shared the tundra with woolly mammoths and saber-toothed cats.\n\n[B] The saiga is an obligate migratory species, undertaking epic seasonal journeys of hundreds of kilometers between summer breeding grounds and southern wintering ranges. During these treks, herds cross treacherous arid terrain, swimming across turbulent rivers and navigating steep mountain passes. Their thick, insulating coat of hollow hairs changes from a sleek cinnamon-buff in summer to a dense, grayish-white fleece in winter.\n\n[C] Tragically, following the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991, saiga populations plummeted by an unfathomable 95 percent in barely a decade. The demise of collective state farms triggered rural poverty, driving local pastoralists to hunt saigas for meat. Far worse, criminal poaching syndicates targeted male saigas for their translucent, amber-colored horns, which are prized in traditional Chinese medicine for cooling fevers and detoxifying blood. Because poachers selectively slaughtered adult males with horns, herd sex ratios collapsed catastrophically—in some areas leaving only one male for every fifty or even one hundred females, causing reproductive collapse.\n\n[D] Compounding poaching pressures, the saiga is acutely vulnerable to sudden mass mortality events. In May 2015, over 200,000 saigas—more than half the global population—died within three weeks in central Kazakhstan. Scientific autopsies revealed the culprit was hemorrhagic septicemia caused by Pasteurella multocida bacteria, an opportunistic microbe that normally resides harmlessly in the respiratory tract but became lethally virulent due to unseasonably high temperature and humidity spikes linked to climate change.\n\n[E] In response to this existential crisis, the Association for the Conservation of Biodiversity of Kazakhstan (ACBK), in partnership with the Frankfurt Zoological Society and the Kazakh government, launched the Altyn Dala Conservation Initiative. Armed ranger patrols were deployed with all-terrain vehicles, drones, and satellite telemetry collars to intercept poachers and map critical migration corridors. Furthermore, new protected areas covering millions of hectares were gazetted to safeguard calving grounds and freshwater streams.\n\n[F] The concerted conservation efforts have achieved one of the most astonishing wildlife recoveries in modern history. Strict law enforcement, community-based ranger employment, and habitat connectivity have allowed Kazakh saiga numbers to rebound from fewer than 50,000 individuals in the early 2000s to over 1.9 million today, demonstrating that dedicated international collaboration can rescue even critically endangered species from the brink of oblivion.",
