      "passage": "[A] For over a century, the orthodox narrative of human evolution depicted our lineage as a clean, unilinear progression from primitive hominins to modern Homo sapiens. Under this traditional 'Out of Africa' model, anatomically modern humans evolved exclusively in East Africa approximately 200,000 years ago, developed advanced symbolic culture in a sudden cognitive explosion around 50,000 years ago, and subsequently swept across Eurasia, swiftly replacing archaic hominins like the Neanderthals without interbreeding.\n\n[B] Over the past two decades, however, revolutionary breakthroughs in paleogenomics, high-precision radiometric dating, and computerized fossil reconstruction have shattered this tidy paradigm. The emergence of modern humanity is now recognized as a deeply complex, pan-African evolutionary mosaic accompanied by recurrent genetic interbreeding across Eurasia.\n\n[C] In 2017, an international team led by paleoanthropologist Jean-Jacques Hublin re-excavated the Moroccan cave site of Jebel Irhoud. Using thermoluminescence dating on burnt flint tools found alongside fossil skull fragments, the researchers established that these early Homo sapiens remains date to approximately 300,000 years ago—pushing back the dawn of our species by an astonishing 100,000 years. Crucially, the discovery located across the continent in North Africa demonstrated that Homo sapiens did not arise in an isolated East African 'cradle', but evolved across connected populations throughout the African continent.\n\n[D] Simultaneously, paleogenomic sequencing led by Svante Pääbo at the Max Planck Institute revealed that modern humans interbred with Neanderthals and Denisovans. Nearly all non-African living humans carry between 1 and 2 percent Neanderthal DNA, inheriting genetic variants that influence immune response, keratin production, and lipid metabolism. In Melanesian and Indigenous Australian populations, up to 4 to 6 percent of the genome derives from the mysterious Denisovans.\n\n[E] Furthermore, archaeological discoveries in southern Africa have dismantled the theory of a sudden 'cognitive revolution' at 50,000 years ago. At Blombos Cave in South Africa, excavators uncovered engraved ochre slabs bearing geometric cross-hatch designs, perforated shell beads used for personal ornamentation, and bone awls dating back over 75,000 to 100,000 years, proving that symbolic cognition and behavioral modernity evolved gradually over hundreds of thousands of years.\n\n[F] The revised scientific consensus portrays human evolutionary history not as a simple tree with a single trunk, but as an intricately braided river—a shifting network of diverse hominin populations that periodically diverged, developed distinct adaptations, and repeatedly merged, exchanging both genes and cultural innovations.",
