        {
          "id": "cam21_t3_p1",
          "title": "Passage 1: Saving the saiga",
          "wordCount": 730,
          "content": "[A] Across the vast, windswept steppes of Central Asia roams one of Earth's most peculiar and ancient creatures: the saiga antelope (Saiga tatarica). Instantly recognizable by its bulbous, downward-pointing inflatable nose—an extraordinary anatomical adaptation that filters out blowing dust during arid summer migrations and warms frigid sub-zero air before it reaches the lungs in brutal winters—the saiga has survived since the Ice Age, when it shared the tundra with woolly mammoths and saber-toothed cats.\n\n[B] The saiga is an obligate migratory species, undertaking epic seasonal journeys of hundreds of kilometers between summer breeding grounds and southern wintering ranges. During these treks, herds cross treacherous arid terrain, swimming across turbulent rivers and navigating steep mountain passes. Their thick, insulating coat of hollow hairs changes from a sleek cinnamon-buff in summer to a dense, grayish-white fleece in winter.\n\n[C] Tragically, following the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991, saiga populations plummeted by an unfathomable 95 percent in barely a decade. The demise of collective state farms triggered rural poverty, driving local pastoralists to hunt saigas for meat. Far worse, criminal poaching syndicates targeted male saigas for their translucent, amber-colored horns, which are prized in traditional Chinese medicine for cooling fevers and detoxifying blood. Because poachers selectively slaughtered adult males with horns, herd sex ratios collapsed catastrophically—in some areas leaving only one male for every fifty or even one hundred females, causing reproductive collapse.\n\n[D] Compounding poaching pressures, the saiga is acutely vulnerable to sudden mass mortality events. In May 2015, over 200,000 saigas—more than half the global population—died within three weeks in central Kazakhstan. Scientific autopsies revealed the culprit was hemorrhagic septicemia caused by Pasteurella multocida bacteria, an opportunistic microbe that normally resides harmlessly in the respiratory tract but became lethally virulent due to unseasonably high temperature and humidity spikes linked to climate change.\n\n[E] In response to this existential crisis, the Association for the Conservation of Biodiversity of Kazakhstan (ACBK), in partnership with the Frankfurt Zoological Society and the Kazakh government, launched the Altyn Dala Conservation Initiative. Armed ranger patrols were deployed with all-terrain vehicles, drones, and satellite telemetry collars to intercept poachers and map critical migration corridors. Furthermore, new protected areas covering millions of hectares were gazetted to safeguard calving grounds and freshwater streams.\n\n[F] The concerted conservation efforts have achieved one of the most astonishing wildlife recoveries in modern history. Strict law enforcement, community-based ranger employment, and habitat connectivity have allowed Kazakh saiga numbers to rebound from fewer than 50,000 individuals in the early 2000s to over 1.9 million today, demonstrating that dedicated international collaboration can rescue even critically endangered species from the brink of oblivion."
        },
        {
          "id": "cam21_t3_p2",
          "title": "Passage 2: Dar es Salaam: The Bus Rapid Transit Revolution",
          "wordCount": 760,
          "content": "[A] Dar es Salaam, the commercial hub of Tanzania, is one of the fastest-growing coastal metropolises in Africa. Between 2000 and 2020, the city's population surged from 2 million to over 6 million residents. For decades, the urban transportation network was paralyzed by chronic gridlock. Commuters relied almost exclusively on daladalas—privately owned, dilapidated minibuses notorious for reckless driving, extreme overcrowding, and arbitrary fare hikes. Commuters spent up to three to four hours each way trapped in sweltering traffic bottlenecks.\n\n[B] In 2016, the municipal government, supported by financing from the World Bank and the African Development Bank, launched a transformative transit solution: the Dar es Salaam Bus Rapid Transit (DART) system. DART is widely regarded as one of sub-Saharan Africa's most successful modern public transit interventions, radically restructuring commuter mobility across the city's primary east-west transport corridor.\n\n[C] The core architecture of DART relies on fully segregated, dedicated bus lanes physically separated from general vehicular traffic by raised concrete curbs. High-capacity articulated buses cruise unimpeded down the center of major boulevards at speeds averaging 30 kilometers per hour, completely bypassing gridlocked car lanes.\n\n[D] Stations are constructed as modern raised platforms built level with bus floors, allowing rapid, step-free boarding for all passengers, including parents with strollers and citizens in wheelchairs. Passengers purchase tickets or scan contactless smartcards at electronic turnstile gates before entering the station, eliminating onboard ticket transactions and drastically slashing boarding dwell times.\n\n[E] The environmental and economic impacts have been profound. Average one-way commuting times plummeted from over two hours to barely 45 minutes, saving millions of collective worker-hours daily. By shifting commuters away from private cars and old diesel daladalas, DART has eliminated tons of localized particulate emissions and reduced vehicular fuel consumption by over 20 percent.\n\n[F] Nonetheless, DART faces acute operational challenges. Rapid urban growth has overwhelmed system capacity: during morning and evening rush hours, stations suffer from crushing passenger queues, and the operating bus fleet struggles to meet skyrocketing passenger demand. Seasonal torrential monsoons frequently flood low-lying sections of the busway near the Msimbazi river basin, temporarily halting operations.\n\n[G] To address these growing pains, Phase 2 and Phase 3 expansions are currently under construction, adding 40 kilometers of dedicated transit corridors and introducing electric buses. Urban planners emphasize that DART's success proves that Bus Rapid Transit offers African mega-cities a cost-effective, high-capacity alternative to exorbitant underground metros."
        },
        {
          "id": "cam21_t3_p3",
          "title": "Passage 3: Rethinking the Past",
          "wordCount": 810,
          "content": "[A] For over a century, the orthodox narrative of human evolution depicted our lineage as a clean, unilinear progression from primitive hominins to modern Homo sapiens. Under this traditional 'Out of Africa' model, anatomically modern humans evolved exclusively in East Africa approximately 200,000 years ago, developed advanced symbolic culture in a sudden cognitive explosion around 50,000 years ago, and subsequently swept across Eurasia, swiftly replacing archaic hominins like the Neanderthals without interbreeding.\n\n[B] Over the past two decades, however, revolutionary breakthroughs in paleogenomics, high-precision radiometric dating, and computerized fossil reconstruction have shattered this tidy paradigm. The emergence of modern humanity is now recognized as a deeply complex, pan-African evolutionary mosaic accompanied by recurrent genetic interbreeding across Eurasia.\n\n[C] In 2017, an international team led by paleoanthropologist Jean-Jacques Hublin re-excavated the Moroccan cave site of Jebel Irhoud. Using thermoluminescence dating on burnt flint tools found alongside fossil skull fragments, the researchers established that these early Homo sapiens remains date to approximately 300,000 years ago—pushing back the dawn of our species by an astonishing 100,000 years. Crucially, the discovery located across the continent in North Africa demonstrated that Homo sapiens did not arise in an isolated East African 'cradle', but evolved across connected populations throughout the African continent.\n\n[D] Simultaneously, paleogenomic sequencing led by Svante Pääbo at the Max Planck Institute revealed that modern humans interbred with Neanderthals and Denisovans. Nearly all non-African living humans carry between 1 and 2 percent Neanderthal DNA, inheriting genetic variants that influence immune response, keratin production, and lipid metabolism. In Melanesian and Indigenous Australian populations, up to 4 to 6 percent of the genome derives from the mysterious Denisovans.\n\n[E] Furthermore, archaeological discoveries in southern Africa have dismantled the theory of a sudden 'cognitive revolution' at 50,000 years ago. At Blombos Cave in South Africa, excavators uncovered engraved ochre slabs bearing geometric cross-hatch designs, perforated shell beads used for personal ornamentation, and bone awls dating back over 75,000 to 100,000 years, proving that symbolic cognition and behavioral modernity evolved gradually over hundreds of thousands of years.\n\n[F] The revised scientific consensus portrays human evolutionary history not as a simple tree with a single trunk, but as an intricately braided river—a shifting network of diverse hominin populations that periodically diverged, developed distinct adaptations, and repeatedly merged, exchanging both genes and cultural innovations."
        }
