      "wordCount": 720,
      "passage": "[A] Lake Victoria, spanning Kenya, Uganda, and Tanzania, is Africa's largest freshwater lake and the lifeblood of millions of riparian inhabitants who depend on its fisheries for daily sustenance. In the late 1980s, an ecological catastrophe struck the lake: the rapid proliferation of water hyacinth (Pontederia crassipes), an aggressively invasive South American aquatic plant.\n\nFloating in thick, impenetrable mats across bays and shorelines, the weed choked municipal water intake pipes, suffocated fish populations by blocking sunlight and depleting dissolved oxygen, and grounded wooden fishing dhows, crippling the livelihoods of local fishermen.\n\n[B] Decades of mechanical clearing and herbicide applications proved futile or environmentally hazardous. However, innovative local entrepreneurs in Kisumu, Kenya, have pioneered an ingenious circular bioeconomy solution: harvesting the invasive weed to produce clean, renewable biogas for cooking and electricity.\n\n[C] In rural villages, women and children traditionally spent four to five hours daily foraging for firewood in depleting forests, or spent substantial cash purchasing charcoal and paraffin kerosene. Indoor burning of firewood generates dense smoke that causes chronic respiratory illnesses and eye infections among families.\n\n[D] Under the community biogas initiative, harvested water hyacinth is shredded and blended in a 3-to-1 ratio with cow dung inside sealed anaerobic digester tanks. The cow dung provides essential methanogenic bacteria that kickstart the fermentation process.\n\nInside the oxygen-free chambers, anaerobic microbes digest the organic biomass, generating a high-energy gas mixture comprising approximately 60 percent methane and 40 percent carbon dioxide. The biogas is channeled through flexible pipes directly into household kitchen stoves, delivering a clean, smokeless blue flame for boiling water and cooking traditional maize porridge.\n\n[E] The socio-economic benefits have been transformative. By adopting hyacinth biogas, families save valuable time formerly squandered on gathering firewood, and save significant money previously spent on charcoal. Furthermore, the byproduct remaining after anaerobic digestion is an odorless, nutrient-rich bio-slurry that farmers apply as organic fertilizer, doubling crop yields for vegetables and bananas without purchasing expensive commercial chemical fertilizers.",
