      "wordCount": 740,
      "passage": "[A] With over 1.4 billion citizens, India is one of the most linguistically diverse nations on the planet. The People's Linguistic Survey of India documented over 780 distinct languages and thousands of dialects spoken across the subcontinent, written in dozens of indigenous scripts. Rather than being an anomalous exception, everyday multilingualism is the fundamental norm of Indian social life.\n\n[B] In typical Indian urban centers, it is standard for an individual to navigate three or four languages daily with effortless fluidity. A citizen in Bengaluru or Mumbai may speak a regional mother tongue (like Kannada or Marathi) at home, converse in Hindi at the vegetable bazaar, conduct business in English at the office, and understand Sanskrit or Urdu through religious broadcasts and Bollywood cinema.\n\n[C] Sociolinguists classify this dynamic as 'functional polyglossia', where different languages occupy distinct situational domains without competing directly. Code-switching—shifting seamlessly between languages within a single sentence—is not viewed as cognitive confusion or linguistic deficit; rather, it serves as a sophisticated marker of cultural identity, social nuance, and urban sophistication.\n\n[D] However, India's linguistic mosaic faces intense modern pressures. Monolingual state education policies, bureaucratic centralization, and the pervasive hegemony of English as the language of corporate power and digital technology threaten minor and tribal languages with extinction. Over the past fifty years, an estimated 250 Indian languages have ceased to be spoken.\n\n[E] In response, educational reformers and cognitive neuroscientists are championing mother-tongue-based multilingual education (MTB-MLE). Research demonstrates that children who acquire early literacy in their native mother tongue develop stronger cognitive abstraction skills, learn subsequent languages (including English) far more rapidly, and show lower school dropout rates than children forced into early foreign-language immersion.\n\n[F] Furthermore, grassroots digital initiatives are deploying artificial intelligence and speech-recognition technology to preserve endangered tongues. Projects like AI4Bharat are building open-source language models for under-resourced Indian languages, allowing rural villagers to access e-governance, banking, and medical telemedicine in their native spoken idioms.",
