      "level": "Band 7.0 - 8.5",
      "duration": "20 phút",
      "wordCount": 730,
      "passage": "[A] In an unassuming studio tucked away in Stoke Newington, north London, Peter Bellerby and his team of master craftspeople practice one of the world's rarest and most painstaking heritage arts: handcrafting bespoke terrestrial and celestial globes. In an era dominated by instantaneous digital GPS navigation and satellite mapping software, Bellerby & Co. Globemakers stands as one of the last remaining studios on Earth dedicated to the intricate, months-long discipline of traditional globe-making.\n\n[B] The journey began in 2008 when Bellerby set out to find a distinctive, high-quality globe as an 80th birthday gift for his father. To his dismay, he discovered that modern commercially available globes were either flimsy, mass-produced plastic spheres with glaring geographic inaccuracies, or fragile, exorbitant museum antiques costing tens of thousands of pounds. Frustrated by the lack of quality craftsmanship, Bellerby made the audacious decision to make a globe himself.\n\n[C] What Bellerby naively assumed would take three or four months of weekend tinkering spiraled into a grueling two-year obsession that nearly bankrupted him. The mathematical and physical challenges of globe construction are formidable. A globe is created by applying narrow, lens-shaped paper segments—known cartographically as 'gores'—onto a perfectly spherical plaster or composite sphere.\n\n[D] The physics of stretching flat paper over a three-dimensional curved surface is unforgiving. Paper stretches when wet and shrinks unpredictably when dry. If gores are stretched by even a millimeter too much, landmasses distort and coastlines fail to align across the equator, rendering the globe ruined. Bellerby spent over eighteen months experimenting with different watercolor paper stocks, glues, and moisture levels before mastering the delicate art of gore application.\n\n[E] Once the gores are painstakingly laid onto the sphere without tears or air bubbles, each globe is hand-painted using multiple layers of fine watercolor washes. Artists blend sea pigments to create delicate ocean gradients and hand-letter cartographic labels. The finished sphere is then mounted onto custom-turned hardwood or cast-brass armillary bases, perfectly balanced on hidden roller bearings so that a gentle touch sends the Earth spinning silently.\n\n[F] Today, Bellerby's studio produces hundreds of bespoke globes annually for discerning cartography enthusiasts, Hollywood film sets, heads of state, and institutions worldwide. While modern digital satellite maps deliver pinpoint navigational accuracy, a handcrafted globe offers something infinitely more profound: a timeless, physical work of art that captures the majestic wonder and interconnected beauty of our fragile planet.",
