      "passage": "[A] The modern tennis racket is a marvel of materials science and aeronautical engineering, but its evolution over the past century was propelled by fierce sporting rivalries, amateur tinkering, and radical technological experimentation. For decades until the 1970s, rackets were crafted almost exclusively from laminated layers of ash, maple, and birch wood. While elegant, wooden rackets were heavy (often weighing over 380 grams), possessed tiny hitting areas ('sweet spots') of barely 65 square inches, and were prone to warping in damp weather.\n\n[B] The first true revolution occurred in 1977, when a Bavarian horticulturist and amateur tennis player named Werner Fischer invented the infamous 'spaghetti-strung' racket. By stringing the frame with loose, double-layered nylon strings interleaved with plastic tubing, Fischer created a string bed that gripped the ball and imparted an unprecedented degree of topspin. Unranked players equipped with spaghetti rackets began defeating top international professionals, producing looping trajectory shots that dipped violently over the net. Fearing that uncontrolled topspin would destroy the integrity of tennis tactics, the International Tennis Federation (ITF) swiftly banned spaghetti stringing in 1978.\n\n[C] Despite the ban, the quest for lighter frames and larger hitting areas accelerated. In the 1980s, aerospace composites—specifically carbon fiber and graphite—superseded wood and early aluminum frames. Carbon-fiber graphite allowed manufacturers like Prince and Head to enlarge the racket head to over 100 square inches without adding weight. These oversized frames dramatically enlarged the sweet spot, allowing recreational players and professionals alike to return off-center shots with blistering power and control.\n\n[D] In recent decades, engineers have fine-tuned racket mechanics using nanotechnologies and vibration dampeners. Carbon nanotubes embedded within the resin matrix stiffen specific zones of the frame, preventing torsional twisting when balls impact the outer rim. Modern players frequently customize their rackets by applying thin adhesive lead weights to the frame's head to adjust swing weight, or wrapping specialized cushioned synthetic grips around the handle to absorb impact shock and prevent tennis elbow.",
