      "level": "Band 6.5 - 8.0",
      "duration": "20 phút",
      "wordCount": 750,
      "passage": "[A] For centuries, maritime piracy has captured the popular imagination through swashbuckling folklore of peg-legged captains, buried treasure chests, and skull-and-crossbones flags. In historical reality, piracy was a brutally pragmatic, highly organized criminal enterprise driven by global geopolitical rivalries, commercial expansion, and maritime labor exploitation.\n\n[B] The so-called 'Golden Age of Piracy' flourished between 1650 and 1730, fueled by the collision of European empires in the Caribbean and Atlantic oceans. Spain's treasure galleons, laden with silver bullion mined in Potosí and gold stripped from the Americas, represented irresistible targets for seafarers who faced squalid conditions, brutal floggings, and meager wages aboard merchant vessels or naval warships.\n\n[C] Contrary to the stereotype of lawless anarchy, pirate ships operated under surprisingly sophisticated egalitarian constitutions known as 'articles'. Before setting sail, crews democratically elected their captains and quartermasters, with captains retaining absolute command only during active naval engagements. Articles instituted strict injury compensation schemes—paying fixed sums for the loss of an eye, limb, or finger—and mandated equitable distribution of plundered loot, with captains receiving only double or triple the share of ordinary sailors.\n\n[D] In many cases, pirate crews targeted cargo vessels carrying essential staple commodities like grain, timber, and naval stores rather than dazzling treasure chests. A merchant captain captured off the African coast was often held for financial ransom, while sailors who had endured abusive punishment from their former officers were offered the opportunity to join the pirate brotherhood.\n\n[E] By the 1720s, European colonial powers recognized piracy as an existential threat to trans-Atlantic commerce. The British Royal Navy dispatched heavily armed frigates to patrol sea lanes, while admiralty courts systematically tried and executed captured pirates, hanging their tarred corpses in iron gibbets along harbor entrances as a grim deterrent to aspiring sea-robbers.",
