      "passage": "[A] Deception is an omnipresent facet of human social interaction. Psychologists estimate that the average person tells between one and two lies daily, ranging from polite 'white lies' designed to preserve social harmony to malicious fabrications intended to manipulate financial markets or secure political power. For decades, forensic psychologists relied on physiological polygraphs—detecting autonomic nervous system arousal such as perspiration and heart rate—to unmask deceit. However, polygraphs are notoriously unreliable, susceptible to false positives when innocent suspects experience acute anxiety, and easily manipulated by trained countermeasures.\n\n[B] In recent years, cognitive scientists have shifted focus from emotional arousal to cognitive load. Lying is cognitively far more taxing than truth-telling. To construct a convincing fabrication, a deceiver must invent a plausible narrative, suppress the true factual memory (inhibitory control), monitor the interviewer's non-verbal reactions for suspicion, and maintain internal consistency across repeated questioning without contradicting previous statements.\n\n[C] Evolutionary biologists argue that human verbal deception evolved hand-in-hand with machiavellian intelligence. In complex primate societies, the capacity to conceal food caches, mislead rivals, and forge deceptive political coalitions conferred substantial selective advantages. As human language emerged, the evolutionary arms race between deceptive capabilities and deception detection mechanisms intensified, shaping modern social cognition.\n\n[D] Today, artificial intelligence and micro-expression computer vision are revolutionizing lie detection. High-speed cameras analyze involuntary facial micro-expressions lasting less than a twentieth of a second, while natural language processing algorithms evaluate verbal transcripts for linguistic markers of deception, such as reduced self-references ('I', 'me'), heightened cognitive complexity words, and subtle vocal pitch tremors.\n\n[E] Yet algorithmic lie detection raises profound legal and ethical dilemmas. If predictive algorithms are deployed by border security agencies or judicial systems, how do we prevent catastrophic algorithmic bias against neurodivergent individuals or ethnic minorities who communicate using unfamiliar cultural body language? The eternal battle between truth and deception has entered the algorithmic era, demanding rigorous ethical oversight.",
