      "questionType": "matching",
      "level": "Band 6.5 - 8.0",
      "duration": "20 phút",
      "wordCount": 730,
      "passage": "[A] Elite competitive tennis is as much a test of psychological fortitude as it is of physical endurance and technical stroke precision. During a five-set Grand Slam match lasting over four hours, players experience intense cognitive pressure, emotional volatility, and severe physical fatigue. Sports psychologists have increasingly focused on the cognitive strategies that allow champions to perform optimally under high-stress match points.\n\n[B] A primary threat to peak performance is anxiety-induced 'choking' under pressure. When the stakes are extraordinarily high, players often revert from automated, intuitive execution to conscious, explicit monitoring of their motor strokes. This cognitive interference disrupts muscle fluidity, resulting in tight serves and unforced errors.\n\n[C] Top athletes utilize cognitive reframing to interpret physiological arousal—such as a pounding heart and elevated adrenaline—not as debilitating anxiety, but as positive excitement and readiness for peak exertion. Furthermore, elite players employ structured pre-performance routines to ground their focus between points, bouncing the ball a fixed number of times and aligning their racket strings to regulate respiration.\n\n[D] Another indispensable mental skill is visualization, also known as mental imagery. Neuroimaging shows that mentally rehearsing a flawless topspin forehand or an explosive serve activates the identical motor cortex networks engaged during physical execution, reinforcing neural pathways without incurring physical injury or fatigue.",
