      "passage": "[A] For centuries, historians viewed the Roman frontier—epitomized by Hadrian's Wall in northern Britain and the Limes Germanicus along the Rhine and Danube—as an impenetrable military barricade designed to seal the civilized Roman empire against barbarian hordes. However, cutting-edge landscape archaeology and geophysical magnetometry have fundamentally rewritten this imperial borderland narrative.\n\n[B] The frontier was not a static, militarized iron curtain, but rather a porous, dynamic zone of economic exchange, cultural hybridization, and diplomatic negotiation. Excavations at auxiliary forts like Vindolanda have yielded thousands of wooden ink writing tablets that record the daily administrative and personal correspondence of soldiers, civilian merchants, and enslaved workers.\n\n[C] These frontier documents reveal that local Germanic and Celtic tribes traded peacefully with Roman garrison markets, supplying grain, cattle, and textiles in exchange for Mediterranean olive oil, wine, and bronze tableware. Furthermore, many indigenous warriors enlisted in Roman auxiliary regiments, acquiring Roman citizenship upon completion of their military service and returning home with Roman consumer habits and Latin literacy.",
