      "wordCount": 2190,
      "passage": "[A] The Industrial Revolution, which transformed Britain between 1760 and 1840, was unprecedented in human history. Prior to mechanization, manufacturing was decentralized, manual, and constrained by muscle, water, and animal power. The breakthrough that shattered these energetic limits was the steam engine, pioneered by Thomas Newcomen and dramatically refined by James Watt. By introducing a separate condenser and converting reciprocating motion into rotary power, Watt allowed a steam-driven piston to drive machinery continuously, freeing factories from geographic dependence on rushing rivers.\n\n[B] The new steam technology relied on an abundant domestic supply of coal mined in northern England, Wales, and Scotland. Coal fueled blast furnaces that smelted iron of unprecedented strength and ductility. With cheap, strong iron, engineers constructed bridges, high-pressure boilers, and textile looms, replacing wooden equipment crafted in small village workshops.\n\n[C] This technological transformation revolutionized British textile production. Spinning jennies and mechanized water frames clustered thousands of wage laborers under one factory roof. The factory system enforced rigid division of labour and synchronized shifts, vastly improving the consistency and quality of woven cotton cloth while dramatically reducing unit production costs.\n\n[D] In the early 19th century, the expansion of the railway network, powered by steam locomotives like George Stephenson's Rocket, connected industrial towns with national ports, accelerating domestic commerce and international exports. However, rapid urban influx created severe social squalor in mushrooming cities like Manchester and Leeds. Inadequate urban sanitation and polluted water supplies triggered catastrophic cholera epidemics, prompting public health reforms.",
