      "wordCount": 220,
      "passage": "[A] Archaeological discoveries on the remote Indonesian island of Obi in the northern Moluccas have radically rewritten our understanding of early human adaptation to equatorial rainforests. For generations, anthropologists assumed that dense tropical rainforests were impenetrable barriers to prehistoric foraging groups due to scarce edible carbohydrates and dangerous pathogens.\n\n[B] However, excavations at two limestone caves—Kelo 2 and Kelo 6—demonstrate that anatomically modern humans colonized the island of Obi approximately 18,000 years ago, during the depths of the Last Glacial Maximum. The cave deposits yielded an astonishing assemblage of artifacts, including meticulously flaked stone tools, shaped pig bone implements, and perforated sea-snail shell beads used as personal body ornaments.\n\n[C] Roughly 11,700 years ago, as the Ice Age ended, the climate became substantially warmer and wetter, causing dense tropical jungle to carpet the island. Rather than abandoning Obi, human foragers adapted brilliantly. Excavators recovered heavy ground-stone axes crafted from volcanic basalt, representing some of the earliest arboreal axes discovered in Island Southeast Asia, used to fell trees and harvest nutritious sago palms.\n\n[D] In later stratigraphic layers dating to 3,000 years ago, archaeologists discovered fragments of decorated pottery and remnants of indigenous spice trade networks. Obi was ideally situated along maritime trade corridors where nutmeg and cloves were cultivated, proving that prehistoric islanders were active agents in ancient maritime commerce.",
