      "passage": "[A] Peatlands cover barely three percent of Earth's land surface, yet they store an astonishing 30 percent of all soil-sequestered carbon—more carbon than is contained in all the world's forests combined. Formed over thousands of years in waterlogged, acidic conditions where lack of oxygen inhibits microbial decomposition, peatlands act as planetary climate stabilizers.\n\n[B] However, when peatlands are drained for agricultural expansion, commercial forestry, or peat extraction, water tables drop. Air penetrates the dried organic matter, and the stored carbon rapidly oxidizes into massive carbon dioxide emissions. Furthermore, dried peatlands become prone to subterranean fires that smolder invisibly for months, releasing toxic smog and obliterating wetland biodiversity.\n\n[C] In the Scottish Flow Country and Southeast Asian swamplands, draining ditches have accelerated land subsidence and water contamination. Ecological restoration projects are now re-wetting degraded peatlands by blocking drainage ditches, raising water tables, and re-establishing native Sphagnum moss to reactivate natural carbon capture.",
