      "passage": "[A] The dream of a universal translator—a handheld device that instantly dissolves linguistic barriers between human beings, popularized in science fiction as Star Trek's Universal Translator or Douglas Adams's Babel Fish—is rapidly moving from fantasy to commercial reality. A new wave of wearable translation earpieces, developed by tech startups like Waverly Labs, leverages cloud artificial intelligence, neural machine translation, and speech-recognition algorithms to deliver bidirectional real-time conversation across dozens of foreign languages.\n\n[B] The operational pipeline of an AI translation earpiece involves three tightly synchronized computational stages. First, high-sensitivity beamforming microphones isolate the user's voice from ambient acoustic noise. Second, automated speech-to-text algorithms transcribe the spoken phonetic signals into text. Third, large neural translation models translate the text into the target language and synthesize speech using natural vocal intonation, streaming the translated audio into the listener's ear within milliseconds.\n\n[C] However, translation engineers face formidable technical and behavioral obstacles. In live trials with Waverly Labs's 'Pilot' earpiece, researchers discovered a significant psychological barrier: users felt acute discomfort sharing one of their personal earpieces with an unfamiliar stranger. Furthermore, natural human speech is riddled with idiomatic slang, ellipses, overlapping dialogue, and colloquial metaphors that frequently baffle machine translation algorithms, leading to humorous or embarrassing mistranslations.",
