      "level": "Band 6.5 – 8.0",
      "wordCount": 780,
      "passage": "[A] According to conservationists, populations of around two-thirds of butterfly species have declined in Britain over the past 40 years. If this trend continues, it might have unpredictable knock-on effects for other species in the ecosystem. Butterfly eggs develop into caterpillars and these insects, which are the second stage in a butterfly's life cycle, represent a vital food source for many species of birds, as well as bats and small mammals.\n\n[B] The primary driver behind the historical decline of butterflies was the loss of natural habitats, caused by the intensification of agriculture and commercial forestry following the Second World War. However, in recent decades, climate change has emerged as an increasingly dominant pressure. Rising temperatures and erratic weather extremes—such as intense summer droughts and mild, waterlogged winters—are disrupting the fragile phenology of butterflies, altering their emergence schedules and shrinking the availability of caterpillar food plants.\n\n[C] In response to warmer average temperatures, several mobile butterfly species have expanded their geographic range northward across Britain. For instance, species that were historically restricted to the southern coast have successfully colonized northern counties. Nevertheless, this expansion is sharply constrained by habitat fragmentation. Butterflies cannot traverse vast stretches of monoculture farmland or urban sprawl to reach isolated pockets of suitable breeding habitat.\n\n[D] The Small Blue (Cupido minimus), Britain's smallest butterfly, has specialized habitat requirements. It forms discrete, localized colonies and relies almost entirely on a single larval host plant, the kidney vetch. The adult butterflies emerge in late spring, and in warm summers they may produce a partial second brood. Because kidney vetch thrives primarily on chalk downland and abandoned limestone quarries, the butterfly is vulnerable to overgrowth by coarse scrub and urban development.\n\n[E] In contrast, the High Brown Fritillary (Argynnis adippe) is now one of the UK's most severely endangered butterflies. Unlike species with multiple reproductive cycles, it produces only a single generation per year. Its caterpillars occupy a very specific microhabitat: warm, sunlit bracken litter where wild violets grow. Loss of traditional woodland coppicing and grazing has caused its breeding areas to vanish, confining surviving populations to a few remote valleys in northern and western England.\n\n[F] Conservation biologists emphasize that targeted interventions can reverse these alarming trends. Habitat corridors connecting isolated nature reserves, along with targeted restoration of native flora, have already produced localized population recoveries. Sustaining these conservation programs is imperative if Britain's rich lepidopteran heritage is to endure the challenges of a rapidly changing climate.",
