      "wordCount": 820,
      "passage": "[A] When Professor Mat Upton found that a microbe from a deep-sea sponge was killing pathogenic bugs in his laboratory, he realised it could be a breakthrough in the fight against antibiotic-resistant superbugs. The sponge had been collected from the deep waters of the North Atlantic, hundreds of metres below the surface. In the pitch-black, high-pressure, and nutrient-poor environment of the abyssal seafloor, organisms have evolved unique metabolic pathways and chemical defenses found nowhere else on Earth.\n\n[B] The deep ocean represents one of the final frontiers of pharmacology. Bioactive compounds synthesized by benthic extremophiles have demonstrated extraordinary potency against drug-resistant bacteria, fungal pathogens, and aggressive cancer cell lines. However, harvesting these marine microorganisms is technically demanding and prohibitively expensive. Research teams require specialized research vessels equipped with robotic submersibles, known as remotely operated vehicles (ROVs), capable of withstanding the crushing pressures of the ocean floor.\n\n[C] Just as biomedical scientists are uncovering the pharmaceutical riches of the deep seabed, another powerful industry is casting its eyes downward: commercial deep-sea mining. The abyssal plains are carpeted with polymetallic nodules—potato-sized mineral accretions containing high concentrations of nickel, cobalt, copper, and manganese. These metals are essential components in the production of rechargeable batteries for electric vehicles, wind turbines, and other green energy technologies.\n\n[D] Mining corporations argue that deep-sea extraction is environmentally preferable to terrestrial mining. Land-based extraction of cobalt and nickel often causes catastrophic deforestation, massive toxic tailings dam failures, and severe human rights abuses in developing nations. Mining the seafloor, proponents claim, would provide an abundant supply of energy-transition minerals with minimal human disruption and negligible terrestrial footprint.\n\n[E] Conversely, marine ecologists warn that industrial seabed mining would inflict permanent, irreversible destruction on benthic ecosystems. Heavy mining tracked vehicles weighing tens of tonnes would crush delicate deep-sea sponges and coral reefs that have taken thousands of years to grow. Furthermore, the extraction process creates colossal sediment plumes that drift for hundreds of kilometres through the water column, choking filter-feeding organisms and blocking bioluminescent communication.\n\n[F] Compounding the ecological risk is the problem of industrial discharge. After extracting minerals aboard surface vessels, mining ships dump slurry containing toxic heavy metals and crushed rock back into the ocean. The International Seabed Authority (ISA) is currently drafting the regulatory exploitation code for international waters. Oceanographers urge global policymakers to exercise extreme caution, arguing that we risk obliterating indispensable medicinal species before we have even begun to catalog them.",
