      "wordCount": 850,
      "passage": "[A] There has long been a general assumption that human beings are essentially selfish. We are routinely depicted as ruthless primates driven by evolutionary impulses to compete aggressively for scarce resources, amass personal wealth, and dominate rivals. In economics and biology, the doctrine of the 'selfish gene' popularized the belief that any altruistic behavior is merely disguised self-interest: we help kin to pass on shared genes, or cooperate with strangers solely in anticipation of reciprocal benefit.\n\n[B] However, archaeological and anthropological evidence paint a vastly different portrait of human prehistory. For roughly 95% of our evolutionary existence—spanning hundreds of thousands of years prior to the advent of agriculture—humans lived exclusively in mobile hunter-gatherer bands. Extensive field studies of contemporary foraging societies, such as the Hadza of Tanzania and the !Kung of the Kalahari, reveal that prehistoric life was characterized by fierce egalitarianism rather than cutthroat competition.\n\n[C] In foraging societies, food sharing is an institutionalized moral imperative. When a hunter kills large game, the meat is distributed meticulously and equally among all band members, regardless of who made the kill. Hoarding resources or attempting to assert dominance is met with severe social sanctions, ranging from ridicule and ostracism to exile. Anthropologists describe this social architecture as 'reverse dominance hierarchy': the collective community actively suppresses arrogant or domineering individuals, ensuring that no single person monopolizes power.\n\n[D] This institutionalized egalitarianism was not a naive cultural preference, but an indispensable survival strategy. In an unpredictable Pleistocene environment, individual foraging success fluctuated wildly. A skilled hunter might experience barren weeks without a kill; sharing meat insured everyone against starvation. Cooperation and empathy were actively selected for by evolution, cultivating an innate human capacity for prosocial kindness, empathy, and collective problem-solving.\n\n[E] If egalitarian cooperation is deeply ingrained in human nature, why has modern civilization become so intensely individualistic and hierarchical? The decisive turning point occurred approximately 10,000 years ago with the Neolithic agricultural revolution. The cultivation of crops and domestication of livestock created storable surplus wealth and private property for the first time. Sedentary settlements enabled ambitious individuals to accumulate land, build military retinues, and establish dynastic hierarchies, transforming our cooperative psychology into competitive materialism.\n\n[F] Understanding that selfishness is an artifact of cultural institutions rather than an immutable biological imperative offers profound hope. Human beings possess a dual nature: we are equipped with latent instincts for both competitive self-interest and profound altruism. By restructuring our economic and social systems to reward solidarity and ecological stewardship, we can reawaken our ancestral heritage of collective cooperation.",
