      "wordCount": 1527,
      "passage": "[A] The reaction principle, upon which all rocket flight depends, is fundamentally simple. It is expressed in Isaac Newton's Third Law of Motion: to every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. If high-pressure gas is expelled through a rear exhaust nozzle, the rocket is propelled forward with equal force. While the mathematical formulation was established in 17th-century Europe, the practical application of rocketry originated in ancient China over eight centuries earlier.\n\n[B] The discovery of gunpowder in China during the Song Dynasty (960–1279 AD) provided the essential chemical propellant. Initially, Chinese alchemists experimenting with sulfur, charcoal, and potassium nitrate produced pyrotechnic novelties. By 1232 AD, during the Battle of Kai-feng against Mongol invaders, Chinese defenders deployed 'arrows of flying fire'—solid-fuel rockets lashed to bamboo arrows that flew into enemy ranks, producing terrifying explosions and smoke.\n\n[C] Mongol warriors quickly adopted the technology, spreading rocket weaponry westward across the Islamic world into Europe. In the late 18th century, Indian forces under Tipu Sultan of Mysore inflicted severe casualties on British colonial armies using iron-cased war rockets. Recognizing the tactical potential, British artillerist William Congreve developed the standardized Congreve rocket, which saw widespread action in the Napoleonic Wars and the War of 1812.\n\n[D] However, for centuries military rockets suffered from terrible inaccuracies due to unstable flight trajectories. The modern leap to space exploration was conceptualized in the early 20th century by visionary pioneers like Russian schoolteacher Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, who derived the foundational rocket equation, and American physicist Robert H. Goddard, who successfully launched the world's first liquid-propellant rocket in 1926.\n\n[A] Discovered in the early 1800s and named nicotianine, the oily essence now called nicotine is the main active ingredient of tobacco. Nicotine, however, is only a small component of cigarette smoke, which contains more than 4,700 chemical compounds, including 43 cancer-causing substances. In recent times, scientific research has been providing evidence that years of cigarette smoking vastly increases the risk of developing fatal medical conditions.\n\n[B] In addition to being responsible for more than 85 per cent of lung cancers, smoking is associated with cancers of the mouth, stomach, and kidneys, and causes about 14 per cent of leukaemia and cervical cancers. In 1990, smoking caused more than 84,000 deaths in the UK alone, predominantly resulting from respiratory ailments such as pneumonia, bronchitis, and influenza. Smoking represents the single most important cause of preventable premature death in developed countries.\n\n[C] More recently, epidemiological investigations have confirmed that environmental tobacco smoke (ETS)—commonly referred to as second-hand or passive smoking—presents grave health risks to non-smokers. Non-smokers who live with chronic smokers experience a 20 to 30 per cent higher risk of contracting cardiovascular diseases and lung cancer compared to those living in smoke-free environments.\n\n[D] The mechanisms by which passive smoking impairs health are both chemical and physiological. Carbon monoxide inhaled from sidestream smoke binds tenaciously to haemoglobin in the bloodstream, forming carboxyhaemoglobin and drastically inhibiting the blood's capacity to transport oxygen to vital organs, especially the myocardium. Simultaneously, airborne particulate matter and toxic oxidants promote platelet aggregation and the formation of dangerous blood clots in coronary arteries.\n\n[E] A landmark epidemiological analysis conducted at the University of California, San Francisco (UCSF) synthesized findings from hundreds of global clinical studies. The UCSF researchers concluded that the cardiovascular toxicity of environmental tobacco smoke is substantially greater than previously assumed. Passive smoke exposure triggers biochemical cascades in non-smokers that induce endothelial dysfunction and arterial stiffness comparable to levels seen in active smokers.\n\n[F] In response to mounting clinical evidence, public health agencies worldwide, including the US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), have designated secondhand smoke as a known human Class A carcinogen. This regulatory recognition has catalyzed nationwide smoking bans in enclosed public spaces, educational institutions, and commercial workplaces.\n\n[A] 'Hypotheses,' said the Nobel laureate Peter Medawar in 1964, 'are imaginative and inspirational in character; they are adventures of the mind.' Medawar was defending the hypothetico-deductive conception of science against the traditional, dogmatic belief that scientific advancement arises mechanically from inductive observation. According to the traditional inductive view, scientists collect unbiased observations without prior expectations, and from this accumulation of facts, general laws emerge automatically.\n\n[B] The philosopher Karl Popper shattered this naive inductive model. Popper demonstrated that pure, unbiased observation is an epistemological impossibility. Observation is always selective: it requires a chosen object, a definite task, an interest, a point of view, and a problem. In actual scientific inquiry, the hypothesis precedes observation; it serves as the conceptual flashlight that dictates what phenomena a researcher should look for and measure.\n\n[C] In the classical inductive method, as codified by Francis Bacon in the seventeenth century, researchers were advised to purge their minds of all preconceptions, theories, and personal passions before embarking on research. Bacon warned against 'anticipations of nature', insisting that only through slow, cautious accumulation of empirical particulars could human beings attain genuine knowledge of the natural world.\n\n[D] However, modern history of science reveals that virtually all revolutionary scientific breakthroughs began with daring speculative hypotheses. Consider James Watson and Francis Crick's discovery of the double helix structure of DNA in 1953. Watson and Crick did not begin by conducting thousands of painstaking, unbiased biochemical assays. Instead, inspired by physical intuition and model-building, they formulated a bold structural hypothesis and actively searched for empirical data that would confirm or refute their conjecture.\n\n[E] Once a hypothesis is formulated, the testing stage begins. A valid scientific hypothesis must be empirically testable; that is, it must yield deductive predictions whose truth or falsity can be ascertained through experimental observation. If empirical results contradict the deductive predictions of a hypothesis, the hypothesis is deemed falsified and must be revised or discarded.\n\n[F] Popper emphasized that scientists can never prove a theory with absolute certainty; they can only corroborate it through rigorous attempts at falsification. Every successful experiment merely demonstrates that the hypothesis has survived another critical test. The grandeur of science lies precisely in this relentless willingness to subject our dearest beliefs to the trial of empirical falsification.\n\n[G] Crucially, there is a fundamental disparity between how scientific discovery actually occurs and how it is subsequently reported in scientific journals. When researchers publish papers, they present their findings in a neat, linear, inductive narrative—making it appear as though the data led inexorably to the theory. In reality, scientific discovery is a messy, non-linear odyssey filled with intuition, false starts, and inspirational leaps.",
