      "passage": "[A] For millions of years, Australia's unique indigenous dung beetles evolved alongside native marsupials like kangaroos, koalas, and wombats. These native marsupials produced small, dry, fibrous dung pellets that native beetles had no difficulty burying and processing. However, with the arrival of European colonists in 1788 and the subsequent introduction of millions of cattle and sheep, the ecological equilibrium was shattered.\n\n[B] Unlike native marsupials, a single dairy cow drops approximately ten large, moist dung pads each day. Australia's native dung beetles were completely unadapted to processing these vast mounds of wet bovine excrement. As a consequence, hundreds of millions of cattle pads accumulated across pastures untouched, smothering fertile grazing land and creating an ideal breeding habitat for plagues of bush flies and biting midges. By the 1960s, an estimated 2.5 million hectares of valuable pasture were lost annually under unburied dung.\n\n[C] In response to this ecological crisis, the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organisation (CSIRO) initiated the Australian Dung Beetle Project. Entomologists recognized that the solution lay in introducing exotic dung beetle species from regions where cattle and large herbivores were indigenous. Dr George Bornemissza proposed introducing tunneling beetles from southern Africa and Mediterranean Europe that specialize in rapidly burying wet cattle dung.\n\n[D] Between 1968 and 1984, the CSIRO introduced numerous species of African and European dung beetles into Australia. Before release, the beetles underwent rigorous quarantine procedures to ensure they harbored no livestock parasites or diseases. The introduced beetles burrow beneath the dung pads, carving deep subterranean tunnels into which they roll balls of dung to lay their eggs.\n\n[E] The biological control project achieved stunning success. The beetles' tunneling aerated compacted pastoral soils, drastically improved rainwater infiltration, and recycled organic nitrogen into the soil. Crucially, by rapidly burying cattle dung within forty-eight hours, the beetles deprived pestiferous flies of their breeding grounds, reducing bush fly populations by over 80 per cent across vast agricultural regions of Australia.\n\n[A] The relationship between agriculture and the environment has undergone dramatic transformations over the past half-century. Historically, agricultural landscapes maintained diverse mosaics of hedgerows, wetlands, and small-scale crop rotations. However, the post-war industrialization of agriculture—driven by government subsidies and market pressures—prioritized maximum yield above all else, precipitating severe ecological degradation.\n\n[B] Modern intensive farming relies heavily on synthetic inputs, including chemical fertilizers, synthetic pesticides, and monoculture crop strains. Between 1960 and the late 1980s, the global application of inorganic fertilizers more than doubled. While this intensive regime temporarily boosted food output, it imposed devastating environmental externalities: nitrate contamination of aquifers, widespread eutrophication of rivers and estuaries, and dramatic declines in farmland biodiversity.\n\n[C] In the European Union, the Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) historically linked farm subsidies directly to production quotas. This policy incentivized farmers to plow up ecologically sensitive grasslands, drain wetlands, and uproot millions of kilometers of ancient hedgerows to expand arable acreage. Consequently, vast swathes of European farmland experienced unprecedented soil erosion and topsoil depletion.\n\n[D] In rich industrialized nations, agricultural subsidies also triggered severe market distortions. By artificially guaranteeing high prices for commodities like wheat, dairy, and beef, governments created chronic domestic surpluses—notoriously dubbed 'butter mountains' and 'wine lakes'. Dumping these subsidized agricultural surpluses onto world markets undercut the livelihoods of smallholder farmers in developing nations.\n\n[E] Recognizing these unsustainable ecological and economic costs, international trade agreements and national governments have begun reforming agricultural policy. Reforms are gradually 'decoupling' financial support from production volume, redirecting public funds toward environmental stewardship, rural landscape preservation, and farm diversification into agro-tourism and renewable bioenergy.\n\n[A] Economists and sociologists have long debated the precise mechanism through which formal education contributes to economic prosperity. In neoclassical human capital theory, schooling is conceptualized as an investment that directly augments an individual's cognitive capabilities, problem-solving skills, and economic productivity. By acquiring specialized literacy, numeracy, and technical competencies, workers generate higher economic output, stimulating macroscopic growth.\n\n[B] However, an alternative sociological perspective—known as the 'credentialist' or 'signaling' model—posits that formal education functions primarily as an elaborate screening device for prospective employers. According to signaling theorist Michael Spence, the primary economic value of a university degree lies not in the practical skills acquired in lecture halls, but in the credential's ability to signal innate intelligence, perseverance, and social conformism to corporate recruiters.\n\n[C] In developing economies, the expansion of primary and secondary schooling has undeniably yielded extraordinary developmental dividends. Robust empirical studies demonstrate that female education, in particular, generates powerful multiplier effects: maternal literacy correlates strongly with lowered infant mortality, enhanced child nutrition, and reduced fertility rates, establishing a virtuous demographic cycle.\n\n[D] Nevertheless, indiscriminate expansion of tertiary education can produce acute structural imbalances. When the supply of university graduates vastly outpaces the absorptive capacity of the knowledge economy, societies suffer from widespread 'graduate underemployment' and credential inflation. In many developing nations, highly qualified engineers and scientists find themselves forced into low-skilled service employment, driving youth disenfranchisement and professional brain drain.",
