      "passage": "[A] In the early decades of archaeology, excavations focused overwhelmingly on recovering spectacular treasures and monumental architecture. However, during the mid-twentieth century, a fundamental paradigm shift occurred: archaeologists began to recognize that ordinary, utilitarian material culture—such as broken pottery sherds, flint debitage, and humble bone tools—contained far more rigorous insights into the socio-economic dynamics of prehistoric communities than royal tombs.\n\n[B] To extract systematic data from material culture, archaeologists developed specialized analytical frameworks. The primary classification tool is the Technical Series (TS), which categorizes artifacts according to the physical manufacturing techniques, metallurgical processes, or firing temperatures employed in their production. By tracking variations in technical series across stratigraphic layers, researchers chart technological evolution and resource acquisition networks.\n\n[C] A complementary framework is the Artefact Types (AT) classification, which organizes objects based on standardized morphological characteristics, stylistic decorations, and functional typologies. Artefact types are indispensable for establishing relative chronologies through seriation: because artistic fashions and tool shapes evolve predictably over time, identifying diagnostic artifact types allows archaeologists to date archaeological strata across entire geographical regions.\n\n[D] Moving beyond isolated objects, modern researchers examine Field Assemblages (FA)—the total contextual matrix of artifacts, faunal remains, and botanical seeds recovered from a discrete architectural feature or domestic floor. Analyzing spatial relationships within field assemblages illuminates division of labor, culinary traditions, and differential household wealth.\n\n[E] Ultimately, these analytical tools serve the overarching objective of reconstructing Social Experience (SE). Material culture is not merely a passive byproduct of survival; it represents an active medium through which human beings negotiated status, expressed ideological allegiances, and maintained communal solidarity across millennia.",
