      "passage": "[A] Environmental psychologists have long studied how microclimatic variables—temperature, humidity, air quality, and acoustic noise—influence human cognitive performance. In modern office environments, the ubiquitous introduction of sealed architecture and central heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) systems was initially heralded as the ultimate triumph in optimizing worker productivity and thermal comfort.\n\n[B] However, empirical laboratory experiments revealed unexpected psychological side effects. In clinical trials evaluating information processing, researchers discovered that subjects working in excessively warm, stale, or poorly ventilated air exhibited marked cognitive deficits: they were less able to concentrate, suffered from lethargy, and committed substantially higher error rates in complex mathematical and analytical tasks.\n\n[C] Furthermore, ambient atmospheric conditions exert subtle influences across biological systems. Studies in animal behavior demonstrate that barometric shifts and ambient temperatures directly dictate feeding cycles, mating rituals, and seasonal lethargy. In humans, improper humidity and poor indoor air filtration cause acute respiratory irritation, dry eyes, and the notorious cluster of ailments known as 'Sick Building Syndrome' (SBS).\n\n[D] Acoustic environments also shape psychological vigilance. In open-plan corporate workspaces, chronic low-level conversational noise elevates cortisol levels and impairs working memory. Cognitive scientists argue that designing sustainable, human-centric working environments requires prioritizing natural daylight, operable windows, and biophilic interior greenery over sterile, artificially conditioned boxes.",
