      "wordCount": 246,
      "passage": "[A] The campaign for women's suffrage in Britain during the early twentieth century was not merely a passionate political crusade; it was a masterclass in modern marketing, public relations, and corporate brand management. Led by Emmeline and Christabel Pankhurst, the Women's Social and Political Union (WSPU)—popularly known as the Suffragettes—pioneered promotional strategies that foreshadowed contemporary political advertising by decades.\n\n[B] Recognizing that public visibility was paramount, the WSPU established an unmistakable corporate brand identity. Central to this strategy was a strictly enforced color scheme: purple symbolized royal dignity and courage; white stood for purity in public and private life; and green represented the hope of political emancipation. Suffragettes wore these three colors ubiquitously—in custom silk sashes, tailored fashion ensembles, ribbons, and jewelry—creating an instantly recognizable visual spectacle on city streets.\n\n[C] To finance their expanding political machinery and legal defense funds, the WSPU operated like an entrepreneurial commercial enterprise. In 1908, they launched 'The Woman's Exhibition' at Knightsbridge, selling branded merchandise including suffrage tea, board games, china sets, and bespoke stationery decorated with the tri-color emblem. Furthermore, the union generated substantial revenue through selling advertising space in their high-circulation weekly newspaper, 'Votes for Women'.\n\n[D] The campaign achieved historic legislative milestones, securing voting rights for propertied women over thirty in 1918 and universal suffrage on equal terms with men in 1928. Historians emphasize that the movement's enduring legacy was shaped as much by its brilliant corporate marketing and strategic messaging as by its militant civil disobedience.",
