      "passage": "[A] In the globalized corporate arena, multinational enterprises regularly dispatch expatriate managers on international assignments. Yet, cross-border corporate surveys reveal a sobering statistic: between 20 and 40 per cent of foreign postings terminate prematurely, incurring immense financial losses and damaging client relationships. International HR specialists identify cultural alienation and language barriers as the primary drivers of expatriate assignment failure.\n\n[B] Historically, corporate executives operated under the ethnocentric illusion that 'English is the universal language of business.' However, while senior executives in foreign capitals often speak English fluently, commercial negotiations, supply-chain logistics, and everyday regulatory negotiations involve local middle managers and government civil servants who operate exclusively in their native language. Failure to communicate in the local vernacular creates profound misunderstandings, with major consequences for contract negotiations.\n\n[C] In commercial surveys conducted across British and European exporters, companies that invested in foreign language competency reported significant sales growth in non-English speaking markets. Conversely, firms that relied solely on English sales literature and monolingual representatives suffered declining market share across dynamic emerging economies, including Eastern Europe, Latin America, and the Arab world.\n\n[D] Progressive corporations now integrate cross-cultural immersion and foreign language training into pre-departure preparation. Rather than viewing language acquisition as an optional fringe benefit, progressive CEOs recognize linguistic agility as a strategic commercial asset that fosters authentic trust, uncovers hidden customer needs, and secures long-term international contracts.",
