          "id": "p1",
          "title": "Passage 1: Australian Hotel Management & Benchmarking",
          "content": "[A] The hospitality industry in Australia underwent severe rationalization during the economic downturn of the early 1990s. International hotel chains faced soaring labor costs, declining occupancy rates, and heightened customer expectations. To survive in an increasingly cutthroat marketplace, corporate hotel groups like Australian Hospitality Industries (AHI) were forced to re-evaluate their organizational structures and operational expenditures.\n\n[B] Traditionally, luxury hotels operated under heavy administrative overheads with extensive bureaucratic management tiers. At AHI, operating costs were inflated by an over-elaborate management hierarchy, with individual properties burdened by up to eight layers of supervisory staff separating the general manager from front-line guest service agents. Furthermore, rigid departmental demarcations between food and beverage, housekeeping, and front office prevented flexible labor redeployment.\n\n[C] In response, Southern Australian Hotels (SAH) spearheaded a pioneering operational overhaul. Management introduced systematic benchmarking—measuring internal operational metrics against the world's most productive service organizations. By auditing work practices, SAH streamlined its corporate architecture, eliminating 25 per cent of legacy management positions and empowering self-directed customer service teams.\n\n[D] A central component of the restructuring was the 'Take Charge' program, designed to solicit direct feedback from front-line employees. Front-line hotel staff were granted autonomous authority to resolve guest complaints instantly, without seeking managerial approval, up to a financial limit of $200. Front-line staff proposals were reviewed within 30 days, cultivating a culture of proactive service excellence and boosting employee morale across all properties.",
