          "title": "Passage 3: Automated Modern Newspaper Printing Plant",
          "content": "[A] The newspaper publishing industry was revolutionized during the late twentieth century by the transition from mechanical hot-metal typesetting to fully automated digital electronic prepress and high-speed offset lithography. Modern high-circulation daily newspapers rely on sprawling, computer-controlled printing complexes capable of producing hundreds of thousands of full-color copies per hour.\n\n[B] The production cycle begins in the editorial offices, where journalists and graphic designers compose page layouts on digital workstations. Once finalized, digital files containing digitized text and high-resolution photographs are transmitted electronically via fiber-optic links directly to the printing facility. In the automated platemaking suite, computer-to-plate (CTP) laser imaging devices expose digital page layouts directly onto photosensitive aluminium printing plates, bypassing the cumbersome photographic negative film stage entirely.\n\n[C] In the colossal press hall, towering multi-unit web offset printing presses stand ready. Giant paper reels—each weighing over two tonnes and holding up to fifteen kilometers of newsprint—are transported from automated storage areas by computerized laser-guided paster robots. The outer protective paper wrapping is stripped, and the reel is weighed and scanned before being loaded into the reel stand.\n\n[D] As the paper web races through the press units at speeds exceeding sixty kilometers per hour, it receives four successive layers of ink—cyan, magenta, yellow, and black (CMYK)—in perfect micro-registration. High-speed electronic scanners monitor color alignment and ink density, automatically adjusting ink keys in fractions of a second. At the end of the press, the printed web is folded, sliced into completed newspapers, counted into bundles, and whisked away by overhead conveyor tracks directly into distribution trucks.",
