      "passage": "[A] The sun provides Earth with more radiant energy in a single hour than human civilization consumes in an entire year. Harnessing this limitless solar flux via photovoltaic (PV) semiconductor cells represents the cornerstone of humanity's clean energy transition.\n\n[B] Traditional photovoltaic cells rely on crystalline silicon wafers. When photons strike the silicon p-n junction, they excite electrons, generating direct electrical current. Early solar cells suffered from high manufacturing costs and low energy conversion efficiency (barely 6 per cent in 1954). However, half a century of materials science breakthroughs has pushed commercial silicon PV efficiency past 22 per cent, while manufacturing costs have plummeted by over 99 per cent.\n\n[C] Next-generation solar technology promises even higher efficiencies. Perovskite solar cells, flexible thin-film photovoltaics, and tandem tandem-silicon architectures can capture broader wavelengths of the solar spectrum, enabling building-integrated photovoltaics (BIPV) where windows and roofs generate clean power.",
