      "wordCount": 229,
      "passage": "[A] The control of fire was the greatest step in primitive humanity's rise to civilization. For hundreds of thousands of years, early hominids could preserve fire only by tending smoldering embers captured from lightning strikes. Generating fire at will remained a laborious trial of friction using wooden drills or sparking iron pyrites against flint.\n\n[B] The quest for portable, instant ignition accelerated dramatically with the dawn of chemical science in the eighteenth century. In 1826, an English chemist named John Walker accidentally scraped a stick coated with potassium chlorate and antimony sulfide against his hearth rug; it burst into flame, creating the world's first friction match.\n\n[C] However, Walker's friction matches were volatile and gave off noxious fumes. The subsequent invention of white phosphorus matches by Charles Sauria in 1830 offered smoother ignition, but at a horrific occupational cost: match factory workers, predominantly impoverished teenage girls, suffered from 'phossy jaw'—a disfiguring necrosis of the jawbone caused by inhaling white phosphorus vapors.\n\n[D] The modern safety match was finally perfected in Sweden by Gustaf Erik Pasch and the Lundström brothers in the 1840s and 1850s. They separated the combustible ingredients: safe red phosphorus was applied to the external striking surface on the box, while the match head contained potassium chlorate. A safety match could ignite only when struck against its prepared abrasive strip, eliminating accidental fires and occupational toxicity forever.",
